#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075052 :tutorial/content "Remember that a *unit* fraction is a fraction containing ![](images/cc/ccee5f5be0445cce59832a3def74f395a7d05cc12dc630fb36baf8f3d2d448bd.svg) in the numerator. So, for example, the fractions \n\n![](images/2e/2e38b15a8f3be74e15b87e81264b66571e593de7250dc902aeaa0e7bc1c506bf.svg)\nare both unit fractions, whereas \n\n![](images/d1/d157ce836a54214740d295f545d4c94d8c57d54d92ac7f075ed9b92556c932b1.svg)\nare *not* unit fractions.\n\nIn this lesson, we will use models to divide unit fractions by whole numbers. \n\nLet's use a fraction model to find the value of \n\n![](images/d8/d8815c6716e962ce4aebee24d004d4c591a1bc35c5fdebcd87d26ace61ead7fb.svg)\nWe start with a square model of ![](images/9f/9fcebe90d169f37bbdbf55f9e4ac86ceaf6ea6d573d101f382a12008bb0c5c2a.svg) split *vertically*.\n\n![](images/cc/ccfe0f457342c456200c98cfc46e63bdc1153698282085164eeafae5c2092c45.png)\n\nTo divide ![](images/9a/9ae11798da63cafee6ce5ccf824a7b96f9f7407a4ee445d1431d907c36f5dd1b.svg) by ![](images/db/dbfe30ca4e672459c180bd90f25f84f025162b8ce73260773fb1fbdf39b0a052.svg) we split the above whole into ![](images/6f/6f33d8df0d08ae9bddce540bedf62f46e93e748e4a5b6fd85b1dc626953e44a6.svg) equal parts *horizontally*. Then, we remove any shaded parts that are not in the bottom row.\n\n![](images/79/7940073ba05d3136ee1d465530a60bc3624b7aa06fd7484d30797758a28976d7.png)\n\nFrom the picture on the right, we obtain that the shaded part represents ![](images/42/42accbcb8cbfd14800ac4a27bc87d5ba529ff4173a91499f32705cd41b0c88b0.svg) of the whole.\n\nTherefore,\n![](images/f1/f14a0f0f9efb98bdfe6cf40f4b1bbb2c88dc20272caaf9d604fcc44bce79d53c.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "5efb47fa-47c0-50c8-9c53-0a64bfc732ea" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8924 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
